RAWMSESSION(1)

# NAME

rawmsession - supervise the rawm desktop session

# SYNOPSIS

*rawmsession* [*-C*] [*-c* _path_] [*-v*]

# DESCRIPTION

*rawmsession* is the lifecycle authority for long-lived infrastructure in a
rawm X11 session. It starts one primary process and zero or more supervised
sidecars from *rawmsession.conf*(5).

The primary process defines session lifetime. The stock primary is *rawm*(1).
When it exits, rawmsession terminates all surviving sidecars and exits with the
primary's status. rawmsession deliberately does not respawn the primary:
*rawm* already implements its own in-place SIGHUP restart, and an external
respawn loop would obscure whether the window manager intentionally quit or
failed during startup.

Sidecars are different. They are declared to be long-lived session
infrastructure and are therefore restarted after any unexpected exit, including
status zero. Managed commands must remain in the foreground; daemonizing or
backgrounding makes the parent exit and is therefore treated as a service
failure. The stock sidecars are *rawmstat*(1) and *picom*(1). A sidecar that repeatedly
exits is disabled after five restart attempts within ten seconds, preventing a
broken executable or configuration from becoming an unbounded fork loop. The
session and its primary continue running after such a sidecar is disabled.
When a sidecar exits, rawmsession kills any processes remaining in that
sidecar's process group before starting its replacement. A sidecar must stay
in the foreground; a wrapper which backgrounds work is still a failed
sidecar, and children in its process group are not retained across restarts.
Programs which detach into another process group are outside this supervision
boundary and must not be configured as sidecars.

rawmsession is not a general autostart facility. Programs listed as sidecars
must be infrastructure whose desired state is continuously running. User
applications, agents owned by other subsystems, and one-shot startup commands
belong elsewhere.

Commands are executed directly with *execvp*(3). Shell parsing, redirection,
expansion, pipelines, and globbing do not occur unless a shell is explicitly
named in the configured argument vector.

# OPTIONS

*-c* _path_, *--config* _path_
	Load _path_ after compiled defaults instead of the normal system and user
	configuration files.

*-C*, *--check-config*
	Validate the effective configuration and exit without starting any process.

*-v*, *--version*
	Print the rawmsession version and exit.

*-h*, *--help*
	Print a short usage message and exit.

# SIGNALS

*SIGTERM*, *SIGINT*
	Terminate the managed session. rawmsession sends SIGTERM to the primary and
	all running sidecars, waits up to two seconds for orderly shutdown, then
	uses SIGKILL for any managed process that remains. The supervisor exits as
	128 plus the received signal number.

*SIGHUP*
	Restart the complete managed infrastructure in place. rawmsession first
	terminates the current primary and sidecars, then *execvp*(3)s its original
	argument vector. The rawmsession process ID therefore remains stable while
	*rawmsession.conf*(5) is re-read. This is the session-level reload path.

	Sending SIGHUP directly to *rawm*(1) remains the window-manager-only reload
	path and does not restart sidecars.

*SIGCHLD*
	Handled internally to reap and classify managed children.

# PROCESS MODEL

Each managed command is placed in its own process group. Session termination
is directed at those groups so ordinary descendants that remain in the same
group receive shutdown signals with their owner. Components that create their
own child process groups remain responsible for those children; for example,
*rawmstat*(1) terminates its active status-command groups during normal
shutdown.

The stock process relationship is:

```
rawmsession
|- rawm          primary
|- rawmstat      supervised sidecar
`- picom         supervised sidecar
```

*rawmenu*(1) and *rawmenu-run*(1) are invoked on demand and are not sidecars.
*pinentry-rawmenu*(1) is owned by gpg-agent's Pinentry lifecycle. *rawlock*(1)
is an invoked authentication boundary rather than a session daemon.

# SESSION STARTUP

For an *xinit*(1) session, make rawmsession the final foreground process:

```
exec rawmsession
```

Do not separately background the stock rawmstat or Picom sidecars in the same
startup file; doing so creates duplicate lifecycle authorities.

# EXIT STATUS

When the primary exits normally, rawmsession returns its exit status. If the
primary is terminated by a signal, rawmsession returns 128 plus that signal
number after sidecar cleanup.

A supervisor SIGTERM or SIGINT similarly returns 128 plus the signal number.
A successful SIGHUP restart does not return. Configuration or startup failures
return non-zero.

# FILES

_${SYSCONFDIR}/rawm/rawmsession.conf_
	System configuration, normally _/etc/rawm/rawmsession.conf_.

_$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/rawm/rawmsession.conf_
	Per-user override when *XDG_CONFIG_HOME* is absolute.

_$HOME/.config/rawm/rawmsession.conf_
	Per-user override when an absolute *XDG_CONFIG_HOME* is not set.

# SEE ALSO

*rawmsession.conf*(5), *rawm*(1), *rawmstat*(1), *rawmctl*(1), *rawmenu*(1),
*picom*(1), *xinit*(1)
